  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The number of statement(s) correct from the following for Copper is/are</p><p>(A) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math> complexes are always paramagnetic</p><p>(B) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;complexes are generally colourless</p><p>(C) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math> is easily oxidized</p><p>(D) In Fehling solution, the active reagent has&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mfenced></math></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;have fulfilled d-subshell and does not have any unpaired electrons so, its complexes will be colourless mostly. and it will be diamagnetic. But&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;has one unpaired electron, so, it will be paramagnetic.&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> are correct and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math> is incorrect because Fehling solution has&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi>II</mi></mfenced></math></p>"
  }
}